# The degree of a Kummer extension generated by a subgroup of `kˣ`

Let `k` be a field containing a primitive `p`-th root of unity `ζ` (`p` a prime),
let `K'` be a separable closure of `k`, and let `T ≤ kˣ` be a subgroup.  Write

`k(T^{1/p}) = k(x : x ^ p ∈ T) ⊆ K'`

for the subfield of `K'` generated over `k` by the `p`-th roots of the elements of `T`.

Kummer theory says that `k(T^{1/p})` is a Galois extension of `k` whose degree is the
index of `(kˣ)^p` in `T · (kˣ)^p`.  This file proves that statement:

`Module.finrank k (k(T^{1/p})) = [T·(kˣ)^p : (kˣ)^p]`.

The proof is the standard one: the Kummer pairing

  `Gal(k(T^{1/p})/k) × T·(kˣ)^p/(kˣ)^p → μ_p`,  `(σ, c) ↦ σ(c^{1/p})/c^{1/p}`

is nondegenerate on both sides, and duality for finite abelian groups of exponent `p`
(`CommGroup.card_monoidHom_of_hasEnoughRootsOfUnity`) turns nondegeneracy into the
equality `#Gal = #(T·(kˣ)^p/(kˣ)^p)`.  Since `L/k` is Galois, `#Gal = [L : k]`.
-/
